{# Shared image for the pdf and check-access services. Both drive Chromium from
   Node, and both used to install it at run time -- roughly twelve seconds on
   every single invocation, paid once per file. Building it instead lets the
   container engine cache the layers, so the cost lands on the first run and
   never again. #}
FROM {{ node_image }}

# Puppeteer's bundled Chromium download has no musl build, and pa11y drives the
# same browser anyway, so both use the distro package.
ENV PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=true
ENV PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium-browser
ENV NPM_CONFIG_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=false

# Resolve `require("puppeteer")` and the pa11y binary from the tree installed
# below rather than from a node_modules directory inside the mounted workspace.
#
# A LOCAL INSTALL, WHICH IS WHY THESE TWO PATHS LOOK LIKE THIS. `npm ci` has no
# `--global`, so /opt/tools is no longer a global prefix and the layout it used
# -- lib/node_modules for modules, bin for binaries -- is gone with it. Modules
# are now at node_modules and binaries at the node_modules/.bin symlinks npm
# writes itself, so neither line needs a hand-made symlink and both come from
# stencil/pipeline.py rather than being spelled out here.
ENV NODE_PATH={{ browser_node_modules }}
ENV PATH={{ browser_node_modules }}/.bin:$PATH

# CHROMIUM IS DELIBERATELY NOT PINNED, and the reasoning is worth keeping here
# rather than in a commit message, because the obvious "fix" makes things worse.
#
# Alpine's repository holds exactly ONE version of a package per branch and
# drops it the moment it is superseded. A pin is therefore not a pin; it is a
# countdown. Measured on this image's own base:
#
#     $ apk add --no-cache chromium=151.0.7716.0-r0
#     ERROR: unable to select packages:
#       chromium-152.0.7977.82-r0:
#         breaks: world[chromium=151.0.7716.0-r0]
#
# That is the version this image installed one release ago, already unusable.
# The fuzzy spelling `chromium=~152` does hold the major and does resolve today
# -- and fails exactly that way the week Alpine moves to 153.
#
# Either one converts silent drift into a hard build failure with no escape
# hatch on the consumer's side: the Makefile and the compose file build this
# image with no build argument, and stencil rewrites both on the next
# `make gen`, so a course repository would be stuck until a stencil release
# landed. A handout nobody can build is worse than one whose page breaks moved
# -- and where the page breaks land is measured directly, on every pull
# request, by the container tier in tests/test_pdf.py and the PDF/UA suite.
#
# What stencil CAN pin is the Alpine branch this installs from, which is the
# base image above.
#
# The asymmetry -- Node pinned exactly, Chromium floating -- is the right way
# round for a second reason. Chromium runs here with --no-sandbox, and it is
# the process that actually parses and renders the content, so leaving it on
# the distro package means every fresh build picks up Alpine's patches for it.
# Node is pinned and therefore does not, but Node here only drives local
# tooling over locally generated, self-contained HTML.
#
# Measured, because the reason usually given for --no-sandbox is the wrong one:
# this image sets no USER and neither service sets a compose `user`, so
# `id` inside it reports uid=0(root). Chromium refuses to enable its own
# sandbox as root. It is not that an unprivileged container cannot sandbox --
# it is that nothing here is unprivileged.
#
# font-noto covers characters outside the latin/latin-ext faces the page
# inlines. With no real font installed, Chromium lays out at fallback metrics
# and every page break moves.
RUN apk add --no-cache chromium font-noto ttf-dejavu

# pdf-lib is used after printing, to add the role map and XMP metadata that
# PDF/UA-1 requires and Chromium does not write. See html-to-pdf.js.
#
# INSTALLED FROM A LOCKFILE, NOT FROM A LIST OF NAMES. Installing
# `pa11y pdf-lib puppeteer` at exact versions fixed those three and the exact
# puppeteer-core puppeteer declares -- and left every one of the other packages
# in the resolved tree to a range, re-resolved on each build, verified by
# nothing (stn-5hv). The lockfile beside this file pins all of them by sha512,
# and `npm ci` checks each tarball against its hash: a rebuild months from now
# installs the same bytes or fails.
#
# No version is named in this comment on purpose. They live in
# stencil/pipeline.py and are rendered in below; a number repeated in prose goes
# stale the first time a pin moves, and says nothing while it does.
#
# The manifest is written here rather than shipped because it is DERIVED from
# the pins in stencil/pipeline.py -- one place where a version is written down,
# and one more file a consumer would otherwise have in their package directory
# with nothing to say about it. `npm ci` needs both and refuses when they
# disagree, which is what makes a bumped pin with a stale lockfile a loud
# failure instead of a quiet one.
RUN mkdir -p {{ browser_tools_dir }} \
 && printf '%s\n' '{{ browser_manifest }}' > {{ browser_tools_dir }}/package.json
COPY {{ browser_lockfile_name }} {{ browser_tools_dir }}/package-lock.json

# AND THE DIGEST SAYS WHAT A SUCCESSFUL `npm ci` DOES NOT (stn-egv). The COPY
# above takes the lockfile out of the build context -- `context: .` in the
# compose file, which is the generated package directory the consumer owns and
# edits. `npm ci` then fetches whatever host each `resolved` names and checks
# `integrity` against a value in that same file, so a consumer-editable file
# chose which bytes became the puppeteer, pa11y and pdf-lib that `make pdf` and
# `make check-access` run as uid 0 over the read-write mount. --ignore-scripts
# does not touch it, for the reason stn-qge gives: nothing has to run at install
# time, because the payload runs when puppeteer or pa11y runs. Measured before
# the fix, with one `resolved` host changed and nothing else: npm requested that
# host.
#
# So this carries the sha256 of the lockfile `stencil gen` wrote -- rendered
# from the same bytes by pipeline.lockfile_digest, never written down, so a
# re-vendor moves the file and the digest together -- and refuses anything else.
# It is the stronger half of what tests/test_pins.py already demands of
# stencil's own copy, where every `resolved` must be a registry.npmjs.org URL
# for the package it claims to be: one hash covers every URL, every integrity
# value, and the shape of the dependency graph, all at once.
#
# AFTER THE COPY, BEFORE THE INSTALL. format-md verifies its copy rather than
# the original because its source sits in a bind mount the host can rewrite
# between the check and the `cp`. There is no such window to close here: a RUN
# cannot read the build context at all, and COPY has already snapshotted the
# bytes into a layer nothing outside the build can reach. The placement is the
# same and the property is the same -- what is hashed is exactly what npm reads
# -- but it is arrived at from the other side. The property to preserve is that
# nothing reintroduces a window: no `RUN --mount=type=bind` here, and no
# `podman build -v`, either of which would put a host-writable path back under
# the check.
#
# ITS OWN RUN, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE ONE YOU FIRST REACH FOR. "The COPY
# layer's cache key includes the file's content, so a tampered lockfile misses
# the cache" is true of both engines today and is an argument about how they
# derive cache keys, which rots. The engine-independent one: a FAILED RUN IS
# NEVER COMMITTED, so no cache entry for one can exist. A cache hit on this
# instruction therefore requires both the same parent layer -- the same lockfile
# bytes -- and the same command text -- the same digest; it replays a pairing
# that previously PASSED. The guard being skipped and the guard being satisfied
# are the same event. Folding this into the `npm ci` line below would be
# fail-safe by construction rather than by that argument, and was rejected only
# so this reasoning has somewhere to live; if you fold it, keep the argument.
# The exception either way is an imported registry cache, which BuildKit trusts
# without re-execution -- nothing stencil generates uses `cache_from`.
#
# AFTER `apk add chromium`, DELIBERATELY. A tampered package therefore pays for
# that layer before being refused. That is cost, not exposure -- nothing hostile
# has run -- and moving the COPY above it would invalidate the Chromium layer on
# every re-vendor, which is minutes on every build to save seconds on a build
# that is about to fail anyway.
#
# `>/dev/null` WITHOUT `2>&1`. Measured on the pinned image: a match prints `OK`
# on stdout, a mismatch prints sha256sum's WARNING on stderr, an absent file
# prints `can't open`, and an absent sha256sum gives `sh: not found` and exit
# 127. All four are fail-closed, so this never passes for the wrong reason --
# but discarding stderr would make all four print the refusal below, and a base
# image that broke sha256sum would then tell the consumer their lockfile is
# wrong and send them to re-run `stencil gen` forever over a correct file.
# Keeping stderr costs nothing and leaks nothing: that line names no digest.
#
# THE COMMENTARY STAYS ABOVE THE RUN. The Dockerfile parser deletes a `#` line
# inside a `\`-continued instruction and /bin/sh never sees it, with no warning
# -- so a comment moved down into the block silently changes the command that
# gets built. tests/test_pins.py asserts there is none.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE. A digest is a checksum, not a signature: it proves
# two files in this directory agree with each other, and both are files whoever
# edited the lockfile could edit. Anyone who changes one and recomputes the
# other passes this check. It is still worth having, because it refuses every
# edit that touches only the lockfile -- a script, a dependency bot, a
# half-finished hand edit, a bad merge -- which is what a `resolved` URL in a
# generated artifact attracts, since it reads as data rather than as a choice of
# what code to run.
#
# AND IT IS ONLY REACHED WHEN THE BUILD ACTUALLY USES THIS FILE. The generated
# Makefile invokes compose with no `-f`, so a `docker-compose.override.yml`
# beside it, or a `.env` setting `COMPOSE_FILE`, selects a different compose
# file and with it a different Dockerfile entirely -- without touching either
# file this guard is about. That is stn-qli, and it is not closed here. Do not
# read this guard as meaning the browser build path is shut.
RUN if ! echo "{{ browser_lockfile_digest }}  {{ browser_tools_dir }}/package-lock.json" \
      | sha256sum -c >/dev/null; then \
      echo "Dockerfile.browser: {{ browser_lockfile_name }} is not the file stencil generated." >&2; \
      echo "That file is stencil's. It is the lockfile this image installs from," >&2; \
      echo "and editing it has no supported effect: a changed 'resolved' URL would" >&2; \
      echo "decide which bytes become the puppeteer, pa11y and pdf-lib that run" >&2; \
      echo "here, as root, over the mounted package -- so the build refuses it" >&2; \
      echo "rather than installing from it." >&2; \
      echo "Run 'stencil gen' for this package to restore it." >&2; \
      echo "If you deliberately override browser-package-lock.json.j2 from your" >&2; \
      echo "own templates_dir, 'stencil gen' will not help: the digest above is" >&2; \
      echo "stencil's vendored lockfile, not your rendered one. Override this" >&2; \
      echo "Dockerfile's guard with it, or drop the override. See STENCIL.md." >&2; \
      echo "On Windows, see stn-at4: generated files are written CRLF, which" >&2; \
      echo "changes this digest even though nobody edited the file." >&2; \
      exit 1; \
    fi
#
# --ignore-scripts, and it costs nothing here. A lifecycle script runs as root
# during the build, with network, before any test in this repository gets to
# look -- and a transitive package that GAINS a postinstall arrives as one
# added boolean in a 48-entry JSON diff. The only install script in this tree
# is puppeteer's, whose whole job is downloading Chromium, and
# PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD above already says not to. Measured on npm 11.19.0:
# `npm ci --ignore-scripts` adds all 47 packages and leaves node_modules/.bin
# holding pa11y and puppeteer either way, because bin symlinks are the
# linker's work rather than a script's. tests/test_pins.py freezes the set of
# packages that declare one, so a new arrival is a red test rather than a
# quiet execution.
#
# `cd` rather than `--prefix`, so this does not depend on which directory the
# build happens to be in. It is `/` today, and a WORKDIR added above this line
# would silently change what npm reads.
RUN cd {{ browser_tools_dir }} && npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund

# One pa11y config per theme, each driving the control before measuring.
# chromeLaunchConfig is in both: this image runs as root -- it sets no USER and
# no service sets a compose `user` -- and Chromium refuses to enable its own
# sandbox as root.
#
# pa11y has no --run-before; it has `actions`, which is better here anyway --
# clicking the real segment exercises the same path a reader does rather than
# asserting about a page that was set up some other way.
#
# Both are forced. Leaving the light pass to resolve `system` would measure
# whatever preference the runner reports -- on a machine reporting dark, both
# passes would measure dark and the light palette would go unchecked while
# the check still went green.
#
# Checking one theme would leave the other's contrast unmeasured, and those
# are the ratios nobody has looked at: 0.11.0 shipped a label at 3.88:1 that
# looked entirely unremarkable.
RUN for theme in light dark; do \
      printf '%s\n' \
        '{' \
        '  "chromeLaunchConfig": {' \
        '    "args": ["--no-sandbox", "--disable-setuid-sandbox", "--disable-dev-shm-usage"]' \
        '  },' \
        '  "actions": [' \
        "    \"click element .theme-toggle__option[data-theme=\\\"$theme\\\"]\"" \
        '  ]' \
        '}' > "/opt/pa11y-$theme.json"; \
    done

# THE PDF DRIVER IS BAKED IN RATHER THAN READ OUT OF THE MOUNT (stn-7ki).
#
# It is still GENERATED into the package directory -- that is the build context
# this copies from, so a consuming project overriding html-to-pdf.js.j2 from its
# own templates_dir still gets its version here, and `make pdf` runs
# `$(DC) build pdf` before every invocation, so an edit is never a build behind.
# What moves is where node READS it from, because Node decides CommonJS-vs-ESM
# from the nearest package.json TO THE FILE. At /workspace that is the
# consumer's, and a course package legitimately has one:
#
#     $ printf '{"name":"course-package","type":"module"}' > package.json
#     $ make pdf
#     ReferenceError: require is not defined in ES module scope
#
# which is a parse error before the script's own tools-directory guard, or any
# other guard in it, can say anything. Here the nearest package.json is the
# derived manifest written above, which declares no "type", so the file is read
# as CommonJS no matter what the mount contains.
#
# AFTER `npm ci`, deliberately. This layer changes whenever the script does; the
# install above takes minutes and must not be invalidated with it.
#
# This does NOT make the script trusted where it was not before. A package
# directory whose html-to-pdf.js an attacker can rewrite was already running
# that file as root -- baking it changes which phase reads it, not who may write
# it. What it closes is the separate question of what ELSE in that directory
# gets to decide how the script is parsed.
#
# WHY {{ browser_tools_dir }} IS A SAFE PLACE TO STAND. The script chdir()s here
# before puppeteer loads, which makes this directory and its ancestors --
# {{ browser_tools_dir }}, /opt, / -- the whole of puppeteer's configuration
# search (stn-jeq). They are safe because NOTHING IS MOUNTED OVER THEM: the
# generated compose file declares one bind at /workspace and, for a package with
# an output_dir, one at /out. Neither is under /opt. That is the property to
# check before adding a volume to the pdf or check-access service -- it is no
# longer merely a question of where files are written.
COPY html-to-pdf.js {{ browser_tools_dir }}/html-to-pdf.js

WORKDIR /workspace
